SAS provides several options to output PDF files with a journal format. Here are a few methods:

**Method 1: Using the `ODS PDF` statement**
```sas
ods pdf file="journal.pdf";
ods listing close;
title "Journal Title";
footnote "Journal Date:/dd/mm/yyyy";
proc print;
  run;
ods pdf close;
```
This code creates a PDF file named `journal.pdf` with a title, footnote, and a report generated by the `proc print` statement.

**Method 2: Using the `ODS PDF` statement with a template**
```sas
ods pdf file="journal.pdf";
ods listing close;
title "Journal Title";
footnote "Journal Date:/dd/mm/yyyy";
proc template;
  define style styles.webabstract;
    style bodytext /
      font_size=8
      leading_unit=pk
      font_style=plain
    ;
  end;
  define layout;
    plant layout_row_region=[
      use_flow_position=5
      left_margin=1cm
      right_margin=1cm
      top_margin=1cm
      bottom_margin=1cm
    ];
  end;
end;
ods pdf close;
```
This code defines a custom template using `proc template` and applies it to the PDF output. This allows for more control over the layout and formatting of the PDF.

**Method 3: Using the `ODS PDF` statement with `ODS GRAPHICS`**
```sas
ods pdf file="journal.pdf";
ods graphics / width=8.5in height=11in;
title "Journal Title";
footnote "Journal Date:/dd/mm/yyyy";
proc print;
  run;
ods graphics close;
ods pdf close;
```
This code uses `ODS GRAPHICS` to specify the width and height of the PDF output. This can be useful if you need to create a specific size PDF.

**Method 4: Using a third-party library, such as `spritesky`**
```sas
%include spritesky(settings);
ods pdf file="journal.pdf";
title "Journal Title";
footnote "Journal Date:/dd/mm/yyyy";
proc print;
  run;
ods pdf close;
```
This code includes the `spritesky` library and uses its `settings` options to customize the PDF output.

